    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Okay, that was better. I see how the bradykinin part works now. But I'm still a bit confused about the feedback loop part. If angiotensin II levels drop, how does that affect the release of renin from the juxtaglomerular cells? Does it stop the whole process or just slow it down?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Great question! The RAAS pathway is indeed a feedback loop. When angiotensin II levels drop due to the action of an ACE inhibitor like lisinopril, the immediate effect is a reduction in the constriction of blood vessels, leading to lower blood pressure. However, the decrease in angiotensin II also leads to a reduction in the negative feedback on the juxtaglomerular cells, which are responsible for releasing renin. As a result, the release of renin is reduced, which in turn leads to a decrease in the production of angiotensin I and subsequently angiotensin II. This creates a negative feedback loop that helps to further lower blood pressure. So, while the process doesn't stop entirely, it slows down, contributing to the overall antihypertensive effect of ACE inhibitors.",
      "source": "target",
